  "question": {
    "text": "A resistor 'R' and $2 \\mu \\mathrm{F}$ capacitor in series is connected through a switch to $200 \\mathrm{~V}$ direct supply. Across the capacitor is a neon bulb that lights up at $120 \\mathrm{~V}$. Calculate the value of $R$ to make the bulb light up $5 \\mathrm{~s}$ after the switch has been closed. $\\left(\\log _{10} 2.5=0.4\\right)$"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "\n$1.7 \\times 10^5 \\Omega$\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\n$2.7 \\times 10^6 \\Omega$\n",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "\n$3.3 \\times 10^7 \\Omega$\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\n$1.3 \\times 10^4 \\Omega$",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "$V_c=E\\left(1-e^{-t / R c}\\right)$\n$1-e^{-t / R c}=\\frac{120}{200}=\\frac{3}{5}$\n$\\Rightarrow R=\\frac{5}{1.84 \\times 10^{-6}}=2.7 \\times 10^6 \\Omega$"
  }
}